{-
   `Show` and its bound - Implementation-Storage.md part 7.

   Two separate claims, tested separately because they fail independently:

    - `show` writes the right text. Asserted by formatting into a sink and reading the units back,
      which is how a fixture with no way to compare strings against a golden gets at the characters.
    - `showBound` is an *upper* bound on what `show` writes, which is the contract the whole sizing
      scheme rests on. Asserted as `length(sink) <= bound` rather than against a constant: a bound
      that is merely generous is legal, and one that is too small is the bug this exists to catch.

   **`Show` is usable generically**, which it was not when this file was written. The one helper
   below is `fn (Show(a)) shown(value: a) -> Int` formatting into a local sink, where there used to be
   one copy of those three lines per instance the file could reach - a `&` argument in a generic body
   did not name storage the callee could write back to, and neither did a `&` parameter of a
   constrained generic instance. Those were one fault seen from the two sides, and `Show(Maybe(a))`
   at the bottom of this file is the instance side of the proof.

   Run on both targets. Every literal is ASCII, for the reason `String.yana` gives - `length` answers
   in each target's native unit and those coincide only there.
-}

-- One sink, one constraint, every instance this file can reach. A local `let &` is what a `&`
-- parameter needs, and `a` being a variable rather than a type is the whole of what changed.
import Test

fn (Show(a)) shown(value: a) -> Int:
    let &sink = String.ofCapacity(64 :: Size)
    show(value, sink)
    return truncate(length(sink)) :: Int

-- The first unit written, which distinguishes "True" from "False" and a sign from a digit without
-- needing the whole text.
fn firstShownUnit(value: Int) -> Int:
    let &sink = String.ofCapacity(64 :: Size)
    show(value, sink)
    return String.unit(sink, 0)

-- The bounds, or a sentinel for `Nothing`. `Nothing` is not a failure - it is the honest answer for
-- a type where measuring costs what formatting costs - so it stays distinguishable from a number.
fn (Show(a)) bound(value: a) -> Size = match showBound(value):
    Just(units) -> units
    Nothing -> -1

{-
   `Bool` - the simplest instance, and the one whose bound is the *longer* of two constants.

   `showBound` answers 5 for both, which is what makes it an upper bound rather than a length:
   formatting `True` writes four units into a buffer sized for five, and the unused unit is the price
   of the bound being a constant that folds.
-}
@test fn boolText() -> {}:
    checkEqual(shown(True), 4)
    checkEqual(shown(False), 5)
    checkEqual(firstShownUnit(0), 48)      -- `0`


{-
   The integer path, including the two values a naive implementation gets wrong.

   Zero produces no digits by division, so an implementation looping while the value is non-zero
   writes nothing at all for it. The most negative value has no positive counterpart, so one that
   negates first prints that same number's digits forever. Neither failure raises anything - they
   produce the wrong text - so both are checked by unit count, which catches either.
-}
@test fn integerText() -> {}:
    checkEqual(shown(0), 1)                     -- "0"
    checkEqual(shown(-5), 2)                    -- "-5"
    checkEqual(shown(1234), 4)                  -- "1234"
    checkEqual(shown(-2147483648), 11)          -- Int's most negative
    checkEqual(firstShownUnit(-5), 45)             -- the sign comes first


{-
   `String` - the row where the bound is a *runtime* value rather than a constant.

   Half of part 7's argument, and `optionText` below is the other half: `Just(11)` for `Int` folds to
   a literal once the instance method is inlined and `Just(length(s))` here does not, and neither
   instance had to say which it was. That is what the `Maybe(Size)` shape buys, and it is why a format
   holding one string and three integers still sizes in one addition.
-}
@test fn stringText() -> {}:
    checkEqual(shown("hello"), 5)
    checkEqual(bound("hello"), 5)
    checkEqual(bound(""), 0)


{-
   `Maybe(a)` - the generic instance constrained on its element's, and the shape every container's
   `Show` has.

   Three claims. The text composes: `Just(5)` is `Just(` and a `5` and a `)`, and the inner `show` is
   dispatched at a type this instance never named. The bound composes the same way and stays a number
   when the element's is one - 11 for `Int` plus the six `Just(...)` costs. And it stops being one
   when the element's does, which is the case that matters: nothing here replaces a `Nothing` with a
   guess, so an option around an unbounded type is honestly unbounded rather than wrong.

   `Just(Just(1))` is here because the recursion is the point - the instance is selected for its own
   element, which is what makes this a composition rather than one hand-written case.
-}
@test fn optionText() -> {}:
    checkEqual(shown(Just(5)), 7)                   -- "Just(5)"
    checkEqual(shown(Nothing :: Maybe(Int)), 7)     -- "Nothing"
    checkEqual(shown(Just(Just(1))), 13)            -- "Just(Just(1))"
    checkEqual(bound(Just(5)), 17)                  -- 11 + 6
    checkEqual(bound(Nothing :: Maybe(Int)), 7)
    checkEqual(bound(Just("hello")), 11)            -- a runtime bound, still a number
    checkAtMost(shown(Just(5)), bound(Just(5)))


{-
   The contract itself: `show` writes at most `showBound` units when it is `Just`.

   Checked rather than assumed, because everything downstream depends on it - the format primitive
   sizes its buffer from these numbers and then appends without re-checking, so an instance that
   understates its bound is a buffer overrun rather than a wrong string.
-}
@test fn contractHolds() -> {}:
    checkAtMost(shown(0), bound(0))
    checkAtMost(shown(-2147483648), bound(-2147483648))
    checkAtMost(shown("hello"), bound("hello"))

